===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[proud|Proud]]; [[haughty]]; [[disdainful]].
#* '''1485''', {{w|Thomas Malory}}, ''{{w|Le Morte D'Arthur}}'', Macmillan and Co. (1891), [http://books.google.com/books?id=OSAXAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA52 page 52] (Book II, Chapter IV):
#*: At that time there was a knight, the which was the king's son of Ireland, and his name was Lanceor, the which was an '''orgulous''' knight, and counted himself one of the best of the court; and he had great despite at Balin for the achieving of the sword, that any should be accounted more hardy, or of more prowess.
#* '''1922''', {{w|James Joyce}}, ''{{w|Ulysses (novel)|Ulysses}}'', Episode 14:
#*: Then spoke young Stephen '''orgulous''' of mother Church that would cast him out of her bosom.
#* '''1966''', Eric Walter White, ''Stravinsky the Composer and his Works'', University of California Press (1966), [http://books.google.com/books?id=rZVRysD6FJsC&pg=PA5 page 5]:
#*: Her nephew describes her as 'an '''orgulous''' and despotic woman', and it is clear that he noticed and resented her numerous unkindnesses.
#* '''1975''', {{w|Georgette Heyer}}, ''{{w|My Lord John}}'', Arrow Books (2011), {{ISBN|0099476428}}, [http://books.google.com/books?id=JYSDcytAHRwC&pg=PA14 pages 14-15]:
#*: They knew that my lord of Arundel had grown so '''orgulous''' that he had lately dared to marry the Earl of March's sister, without license.
# [[ostentatious|Ostentatious]]; [[showy]].
# [[swollen|Swollen]]; [[augmented]]; [[excessive]].
#* '''1967''', {{w|John T. Sladek}}, ''Masterson and the Clerks,'' reprinted in ''{{w|Michael Moorcock bibliography|Best SF Stories from New Worlds 4}},'' edited by Michael Moorcock
#*: The smile became an orange balloon, '''orgulous''' and threatening.
# [[threatening|Threatening]]; [[dangerous]].

